# Diagnosing a full disk on Linux

The reflex is `cd / && du -ks * | sort -rn`. On a large or busy box that can take many
minutes, because it walks every inode on the system — including `/proc`, `/sys`, other
filesystems, and any network mount — single-threaded, with a cold cache.

Most of the time you can avoid the full walk entirely. Work down this list in order.

## 1. Narrow the target before scanning anything

The fastest scan is the one you don't run. Find out *which* filesystem is full first:

```
df -h
df -i
```

- If `df -h` shows `/var` full and `/` fine, scan `/var` — not `/`.
- **`df -i` is the one people forget.** If inodes are at 100% while `df -h` shows free
  space, you have millions of tiny files and every size-based tool is the wrong
  instrument. Hunt directory entry counts instead:

```
sudo find / -xdev -type d -printf '%h\n' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head
```

## 2. Fix the du you already type

```
du -xh -d1 / | sort -rh | head -20
```

- **`-x`** stays on one filesystem. Skips `/proc`, `/sys`, network mounts, bind mounts,
  other disks. This is usually the single biggest speedup.
- **`-d1`** stops at depth 1. Descend by hand into whichever directory is large instead
  of summing the whole tree up front.

Repeat into the offender:

```
du -xh -d1 /var | sort -rh | head
du -xh -d1 /var/lib | sort -rh | head
```

On a production box, keep it off the critical path:

```
ionice -c3 nice -n19 du -xh -d1 / | sort -rh | head -20
```

## 3. Use a faster tool

```
gdu -x /                # parallel, interactive — fastest of the three
dust -x -d2 /           # one-shot tree output, nothing interactive
ncdu -x /               # interactive, most widely available
```

`gdu` parallelises the walk and is typically several times quicker than `du` on
high-latency storage. `dust` is best when you want output you can paste somewhere.
See [dust](dust), [ncdu](ncdu).

Scan once, browse repeatedly — useful on a slow box where you don't want to rescan:

```
ncdu -x -o /tmp/scan.json /
ncdu -f /tmp/scan.json
```

## 4. Looking for one big file, not a big tree

Much faster than any `du`, because it never sums directories:

```
sudo find / -xdev -type f -size +1G -printf '%s\t%p\n' 2>/dev/null | sort -rn | head
```

Good when you suspect a single runaway log, core dump, or VM image.

## 5. The cases du can never explain

Reach for these when **`df` says full but `du` says the space isn't there**. Sinking an
hour into `du` here is wasted — it structurally cannot see either case.

### Deleted but still-open files

A process holds an open handle to a file that has been unlinked. The space stays
allocated until the process closes it or dies. `du` sees nothing; `df` sees it.

```
sudo lsof +L1
```

Look for link count 0. Restart the holding process to reclaim. Classic after rotating a
log an application still has open.

### Files hidden underneath a mountpoint

Data written to a directory *before* something was mounted over it. Still on disk,
invisible to any normal traversal:

```
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/root && sudo mount --bind / /mnt/root
du -xh -d1 /mnt/root
sudo umount /mnt/root
```

### Filesystem-level space

On btrfs/ZFS, snapshots hold space that no visible file accounts for — see [btrfs](btrfs):

```
sudo btrfs filesystem usage /
sudo btrfs subvolume list /
zfs list -o space
```

On LVM, check for snapshot volumes filling the pool:

```
sudo lvs -o +lv_when_full,data_percent,snap_percent
```

## 6. Usual suspects — check these before any full scan

```
journalctl --disk-usage                    # then: journalctl --vacuum-size=200M
du -sh /var/cache/apt/archives             # then: apt clean
du -sh /var/lib/snapd/snaps                # old snap revisions, Ubuntu
du -sh /var/log
du -sh ~/.cache
```

Snap keeps old revisions by default and grows quietly on desktop installs. To trim:

```
snap list --all
sudo snap set system refresh.retain=2
```

Containers hold a lot in one place, and the prune is a one-liner:

```
docker system df && docker system prune -a --volumes
podman system df && podman system reset
```

## Quick reference

```
df -h                                # which filesystem
df -i                                # inode exhaustion
du -xh -d1 / | sort -rh | head       # portable, no extra tools
gdu -x /                             # fastest interactive
sudo lsof +L1                        # deleted-but-open (df/du disagree)
find / -xdev -type f -size +1G       # one huge file
journalctl --vacuum-size=200M        # systemd journal
```
